    "description": "Iran’s National Information Network combines domestic communications infrastructure and services with policies promoting local hosting and platforms. Its development is central to understanding how domestic services and access to the global Internet can experience different restrictions.",
    "tags": ["National Information Network", "Domestic connectivity", "Internet governance", "Digital infrastructure", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:08:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:08:00Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s National Information Network: Infrastructure and Control",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s National Information Network, domestic hosting, policy goals, and the distinction between local services and global Internet access."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A network and a policy programme",
        "content": "The National Information Network, commonly abbreviated NIN, is broader than a single telecommunications network. Filterwatch’s analysis of Iranian policy documents describes a programme encompassing infrastructure, regulation, and domestic applications and services. The Persian name, Shabake-ye Melli-ye Ettelaat, translates as National Information Network. For international readers, the important distinction is between the infrastructure carrying communications inside Iran and the connections that make services elsewhere in the world reachable. Developing the former does not, by itself, establish the availability of the latter. [s1]\n\nThe programme combines objectives that need separate evaluation. Expanding connectivity and local hosting can support domestic digital services. Filterwatch also identifies a strategy of increasing authorities’ control over infrastructure and locally held user data. These are different questions: whether a connection exists, which services it reaches, and what protections govern the information travelling through it. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What early technical research established",
        "content": "Evidence of a distinct domestic networking layer predates many recent debates about the NIN. In a working paper submitted in September 2012, researcher Collin Anderson reported that private Internet Protocol addresses could be routed within Iran and that some servers appeared to have both domestic and globally reachable addresses. Private addresses ordinarily identify resources within restricted networks rather than destinations on the public Internet. [s2]\n\nThe paper was explicitly preliminary. Anderson declined to infer the purpose of the observed arrangement, despite its possible political significance. That caution remains useful: a technical observation can establish that a particular route or addressing arrangement exists without proving every policy intention attributed to it. The study is historical evidence, not a map of Iran’s network architecture in 2026. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "availability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Domestic availability and global access",
        "content": "Network restrictions can affect different services and protocols unevenly. Research coordinated by OONI and the Internet Society documented mobile outages, regional disruptions, application blocking, and interference with encrypted domain-name services during the autumn 2022 protests. A user’s experience therefore depended on their provider, location, time, and intended service. Describing every restriction simply as the Internet being either on or off obscures these differences. [s3]\n\nMore recent measurements reinforce the need for precision. Cloudflare reported partial restoration of international traffic in May 2026 following a prolonged shutdown. It also observed that relatively stable announcements of IPv4 address space coexisted with a dramatic loss of actual traffic. An address remaining visible in global routing tables consequently did not demonstrate that people could use the associated connection normally. These findings concern Cloudflare’s measurement perspective and do not establish the availability of every domestic NIN service. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-claims",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to interpret claims of progress",
        "content": "The NIN is best understood through specific outcomes rather than a single completion percentage. A claim about domestic hosting should be assessed as hosting evidence; a claim about international access requires measurements of international reachability. Neither alone establishes privacy, freedom of access, or nationwide service quality. The sources inspected here support this distinction but do not provide a reproducible annual measure of the programme’s overall completion. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
