    "description": "Data centers house the computing, storage, and networking equipment behind digital services. In Iran, domestic hosting providers connect these facilities to telecommunications networks and the wider policy programme of localizing digital infrastructure.",
    "tags": ["Data centers", "Hosting", "Cloud computing", "Colocation", "Interconnection"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:08:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:08:00Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Data Centers in Iran: Hosting, Cloud Services and Connectivity",
      "description": "Explore the role of Iranian data centers, the difference between hosting and colocation, and the limits of public infrastructure inventories."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "facilities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The physical home of digital services",
        "content": "A data center is a physical facility containing the computing, storage, and networking infrastructure used to run applications and manage their data. Cloud services still depend on this physical equipment: the cloud changes how customers obtain computing resources, rather than removing the buildings and machines underneath them. IBM distinguishes facilities serving one organization from infrastructure operated by providers for multiple customers. [s1]\n\nThis distinction helps explain Iran’s hosting market. A company selling a website package, a virtual server, or cloud storage is describing a service. The underlying equipment may be housed in a facility it operates or in infrastructure supplied by another organization. A service brand and a physical data-center site should therefore be counted separately when describing the sector. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-examples",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What provider descriptions establish",
        "content": "Asiatech, an Iranian telecommunications provider, states in its company profile that it has been certified to provide Internet Data Center services since 2013. Its listed services include web hosting, dedicated and shared servers, and colocation. Colocation means housing a customer’s equipment in a provider’s facility; purchasing server capacity instead gives the customer access to computing equipment supplied by the provider. Asiatech’s account establishes its stated service history, but is not an independent audit of operating capacity or reliability. [s1][s2]\n\nHostiran offers another example of the relationship between hosting and connectivity. Its own profile describes a dedicated data center with fiber connectivity to the infrastructure network and lists hosting, virtual and dedicated servers, and cloud services. These statements identify the company’s advertised activities. Promotional claims about size or leadership are not treated here as verified market rankings. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interconnection",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A facility also needs routes to its users",
        "content": "Servers become useful to remote users through network connections. RIPE NCC’s regional research explains how data centers can provide the colocation environment in which network operators interconnect, including through Internet exchange points. An exchange point enables participating networks to exchange traffic; it is a different function from storing or processing that traffic. The report covers the Arabic-speaking Middle East, so its architectural explanation is used here without presenting its regional inventory as an Iranian census. [s4]\n\nIran-specific evidence illustrates this connection. The Internet Society’s August 2026 Tehran IX record lists Hostiran DC Network among the exchange’s participants. The record is based on self-reported PeeringDB information. It demonstrates a documented interconnection presence, not the number of Hostiran buildings, the amount of installed computing equipment, or measured traffic across all its services. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the sector carefully",
        "content": "A credible account of Iran’s data centers must keep service offerings, physical sites, and network participation distinct. The inspected provider profiles offer concrete examples, but they do not form a complete national inventory. Nor do they provide comparable annual measurements of electricity capacity, occupied racks, or independently verified availability. The evidence supports describing an operating domestic hosting ecosystem; it does not support a precise national capacity total or a ranking of facilities. [s2][s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
